{"rule": "Return this JSON object with the rule field unchanged and a new passage of about 250 words in the text field.", "text": "The lighthouse on Keller Point had been automated for thirty years, but Tomas still rowed out every Sunday to polish the brass. His father had kept the light before him, and his grandfather before that, and Tomas could not quite believe that a machine understood the sea the way a person did. The coast guard had given him a key out of politeness, assuming he would tire of the trip. He never did. He climbed the spiral stairs slowly now, one hand on the cold iron rail, counting the steps aloud: one hundred and eighteen, the same as always. At the top he wiped salt from the great lens and listened to the gulls argue on the rocks below. One winter a fierce gale knocked out the power along the whole peninsula. The backup generator coughed and failed, and the lamp went dark just as a fishing boat was feeling its way home through the sleet. Tomas saw the blackness from his kitchen window and did not hesitate. He pulled on his oilskins, dragged the skiff into the churning harbor, and rowed until his shoulders burned. Inside the tower he found his father's old kerosene lantern still hanging on its hook, dusty but whole. He lit it, carried it to the gallery, and swung it in slow arcs against the storm for nearly two hours. At dawn the fishermen came to thank him, their faces raw from the wind. Tomas only shrugged and went back to polishing the brass. Machines, he told them, were fine things, but the sea still preferred a familiar hand."}
